  0. Definitions.

  "This License" refers to version 3 of the GNU Affero General Public License.

  "Copyright" also means copyright-like laws that apply to other kinds of
works, such as semiconductor masks.

  "The Program" refers to any copyrightable work licensed under this
License.  Each licensee is addressed as "you".  Licensees include
individuals and organizations.  "Derivative works" means works based on
the Program or the Program's source code.

  "Source form" means the preferred form of a work for making changes to
it.  For a program, the source form means all source code, documentation
and configuration files.

  "Corresponding source" means all the source code needed to generate,
install and (for a work in the form of a machine-readable executable)
run the object code of the program, including scripts to control these
activities.

  "Interactive user interface" means an interface in which input from
the user can cause the program to perform actions.

  "User product" means either (1) a "consumer product", which means any
tangible personal property which is normally used for personal, family,
or household purposes, or (2) anything designed or sold for incorporation
into a dwelling.

  "Conveying" means any kind of transfer of ownership or control of a
copy of the program.

  1. Source Code.

  The "source code" for a work means the preferred form of the work
for making modifications to it.  "Object code" means any non-source
form of a work.

  A "Standard Interface" means an interface that either is an official
standard defined by a recognized standards body, or, in the case of
interfaces specified for a particular programming language, one that
is widely used among developers working in that language.

  The "System Libraries" of an executable work include anything, not
part of the work, that is normally included in the target system's
distribution of such a library.

  2. Protecting Users' Legal Rights From Anti-Circumvention Law.

  No covered work shall be deemed part of an essential technological
measure for preventing or limiting circumvention of technological
measures that control the use of copies of a work.

